the amount of rubbish in Cairo is quite  staggering the bulk of it comes here an  area known as garbage city and it's  dealt with by the Zabaleen an army of  unofficial workers who recycle more than  80% of the rubbish they're collecting  making them the top recyclers in the  world even so the amount of rubbish is  overwhelming look at this Cairo skyline  there are piles and piles of rubbish  stacked up on the city's rooftops the  rubbish and the huge amount of plastic  waste ends up everywhere so many of the  tributaries and irrigation canals  leading to the River Nile are utterly  clogged with every type of scrap  imaginable this could well be the future  nightmare scenario of all inhabited  areas along the length of the Nile the  canals seem to have become dumping  grounds as the city's population grows  and the rubbish it's generating grows  with it that leads to entire waterways  being clogged and it will have a  devastating effect on agriculture on  fish and on the livelihoods of millions  of people fish have been a staple part  of the Egyptian diet since the days of  the Pharaohs and the fishing industry  employs hundreds of thousands of people  in the country we wanted to know just  what impact all this plastic was having  so we turned to a top scientist to do  the first research into the prevalence  of micro plastics in Nile fish  dr. Farhan Khan examined a batch of fish  from the Nile and two of the most common  in more than three-quarters of them he  found microplastics when all the samples  were laid out on the bench  you could go through them and seek  really quite big bits of plastic and you  could see really quite big bits of  plastic on a lot of the samples  many of the fish had ingested more than  one piece of plastic and dr. Khan was  focusing only on the larger sections if  anything we probably underestimate  what the problem is that you know the  fact that we've detected visible  plastics ones that you could take  pictures of with your phone for example  so we're not seeing the smallest size  Ranger so that estimate we make of  around 70 to 80 percent is probably on  the conservative side Egyptians are very  precious about the Nile and citizens  have been jailed in the past for  doubting its cleanliness but we found a  great many are worried about the plastic  in the world's longest river which feeds  into the Mediterranean well I do get  frustrated that no matter what I do  personally it's so hard to change  people's habits but people have bigger  problems in their everyday life of just  getting by they don't understand the  damage they do by chucking stuff away  our research is a startling indication  of just how big the problem of plastic  in Nile fish is this is a river at risk  of being suffocated by plastic and it's  only getting worse  Alex Crawford Sky News in Egypt
 

